  "hypothesis": "At one fixed commit, the real ois/oic/oi binaries satisfy every TV-B3-11 vector (a-i) and the regressions TV-B3-02, TV-B3-04 and TV-B3-10 on SQLite 3.54.1 and PostgreSQL 18.6 with zero zero-tolerance events, while the whole earlier vector suite still passes and the quality gates hold (unit coverage of the delivery unit >=95%, race detector, fuzzing, vet, staticcheck, govulncheck).",
  "alternatives": [
    "recovery works on one backend only",
    "a restore silently re-enables revoked access, admits Opens before the administrator resumes, or reuses an old term",
    "export or import loses or alters rows that the digests fail to detect, or a refused import leaves a half-written target",
    "the store reports recovery while writes still fail, or a full or read-only store keeps admitting Opens",
    "congestion is absorbed by unbounded queueing instead of fast refusals",
    "the vectors pass only because the tmpfs or pg_dump/pg_restore paths were skipped"
  ],
  "disproof": "Any required vector failing or skipped on a required backend (including TV-B3-11e on the tmpfs and the pg_dump/pg_restore paths of TV-B3-11a/b/c), any ZERO-TOLERANCE line, any failing earlier vector, a coverage total below 95%, a race report, a fuzz failure, or a vet/staticcheck/govulncheck finding refutes the hypothesis for this commit. Missing or timed-out steps make the run inconclusive, never a pass.",
  "limitations": [
    "Single machine loopback topology on a GitHub-hosted ubuntu-24.04 runner; not the 1C1G target environment (G5) and not WAN latency",
    "Disk full is a 32 MiB tmpfs and a read-only PostgreSQL is default_transaction_read_only; real disks, quotas and storage faults may fail differently",
    "PostgreSQL backups use pg_dump/pg_restore of the server version inside the service container; pg_basebackup and point-in-time recovery stay deployment procedures outside this run",
    "Backup, restore, migration and maintenance durations and sizes are observations of small test databases without pass lines (B4 §6); they are not capacity or RTO/RPO commitments (G5, G6)",
    "No PostgreSQL HA (G6), probes (G2), real path faults (G3) or real compatibility (G7)"
  ],
